        <mxCell id="n20" value="" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;" edge="1" parent="1" source="n7" target="n9"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n21" value="writes images today" style="endArrow=block;endFill=1;html=1;strokeColor=#333333;fontSize=11;exitX=0.96;exitY=1;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="n6" target="n10"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n22" value="upload  (planned)" style="endArrow=open;endFill=0;dashed=1;html=1;strokeColor=#666666;fontSize=11;exitX=0.5;exitY=0;exitDx=0;exitDy=0;entryX=0;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" edge="1" parent="1" source="n6" target="n11"><mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
        <mxCell id="n23" value="Four things this diagram states:&lt;br&gt;1. The API keeps NO state, so it can be replicated. Everything that used to&lt;br&gt;   live in process memory — cache, rate-limit counters, socket lists — is in Redis.&lt;br&gt;   NOT TRUE YET: there is no Redis client in src/. CacheModule.register() and&lt;br&gt;   ThrottlerModule both keep their state inside the Node process, so today the&lt;br&gt;   API is stateful and the x3 above is a target, not a fact.&lt;br&gt;2. Exactly ONE worker. Cron inside the API would run the reconciliation job&lt;br&gt;   three times; for a job that touches money that is a serious bug.&lt;br&gt;   NOT TRUE YET: TasksModule is imported by AppModule, so the cron runs inside&lt;br&gt;   the API. Harmless while there is one replica — and the exact reason a second&lt;br&gt;   replica cannot simply be started.&lt;br&gt;3. Images ARE on the VPS disk today — multer writes to public/images/{folder},&lt;br&gt;   and there is no R2 or S3 client anywhere in src/. That blocks point 1: a&lt;br&gt;   second API replica cannot serve an image the first one wrote. Moving to R2&lt;br&gt;   is decided, not built.&lt;br&gt;4. Admin is its own deployable on its own hostname, so admin code is never&lt;br&gt;   shipped in the customer bundle. Sessions are per-origin, so an admin who&lt;br&gt;   also sells signs in twice.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Checked against the code on 14/08/2026. Red = decided, not built.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The box is now buildable. Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml exist in&lt;br&gt;Zoldify_Backend, and the stack has been brought up from an empty volume and&lt;br&gt;answered on /api/v1/products. Compose starts exactly what the code needs:&lt;br&gt;mysql, a one-shot migrate step, and one api. No Redis, no worker.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The uploads question has an answer now: compose mounts a NAMED volume at&lt;br&gt;/app/public/images, and a file written there survives docker compose down and&lt;br&gt;up — measured, not assumed. That keeps uploads across a redeploy but does not&lt;br&gt;unblock point 1: a volume lives on one machine, so it still cannot be shared&lt;br&gt;by two API replicas. Only object storage fixes that.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;See DEPLOY.md for the commands and for what is still missing." style="shape=note;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;size=14;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=6;fontSize=11;fillColor=#fffbe6;strokeColor=#c8a415;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry x="60" y="700" width="740" height="480" as="geometry"/></mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
